      "finalText": "able, and a later retry worked.\n- **Exchange rate:** rightclick-x's research found ExchangeRate-API in the APIs.guru directory. That search also showed several exchange-rate servers in the MCP Registry, but I used ExchangeRate-API because it needs no key.\n\n**Saved capabilities** (each run end-to-end and verified)\n- **Capital briefing** (`/x/capital-briefing`): looks up the country's capital, then reads the opening section of that capital's Wikipedia article. It takes the input `{country, section_index: 0}` and the output must contain \"capital and most populous city\".\n- **Currency in USD** (`/x/currency-in-usd`): looks up the country's currency, then gets that currency's latest exchange rates. The check confirms the base is EUR and the USD rate is above 0.\n\n**Problems along the way**\n- **Wikipedia summary step:** rightclick-x treats that tool's title input as a web address, so the automatic pipeline always failed on it. The saved workflow reads the article's opening section instead. I ran the summary tool once by hand to get the summary above, and its text matches that section.\n- **Capital briefing:** my first saved version used rightclick-x's built-in summarizer, which picked text from the end of the article. I republished over it with the version described above.\n- **Currency in USD:** my first saved version returned the update timestamp instead of the rate. The republished version returns all the rates, with the USD rate at `rates.USD`.\n- **Leftover versions:** the wrong first versions are still stored in rightclick-x as unnamed pipelines (`composite:484c56324879` and `composite:f0f4440e229c`).\n- **Removed providers:** automatic acquisition added three irrelevant ones (airport search, city context and a \"capital mental\" server), so I removed them.\n\nANSWER: Lisbon | EUR 1.12 | Lisbon is the capital and most populous city of Portugal, with an estimated population of 658,236 as of 2025, within its administrative limits and 3,353,000 within the metropolis, as of 2025."
